Reports fewer than 31/31 under BIT_GC=stress against a Bit-sourced libbitrt.a.
Tracked as #1845, which must remove this file when it closes.

INTERMITTENT, and it is the mask that says what is wrong. Measured over eight
runs: 31, 30, 30, 30, 27, 26, 26, 31 - so bit 1 (B1) and bit 4 (B3) each drop
out on their own and sometimes together (26 is both). Every affected property
has the same shape: a raw port address held across a later allocation.

B1 chains four allocations in one condition, so the first three results are
live only as `int` while the fourth allocates:

    sizeOf(mine("")) == 0 && sizeOf(mine("a")) == 1 &&
    sizeOf(mine("abcdefgh")) == 8 && sizeOf(mine("the quick brown fox")) == 19

B3 is the same hazard, and there it is the property's whole point. B3 proves
two port strings do not ALIAS:

    let x = mine("iiii")
    let y = mine("jjjj")
    if (loadByte(bytesOf(x)) == 105 && loadByte(bytesOf(y)) == 106) { ... }

`mine` returns a raw `int` (ABI.md §2 string header address), so `x` is
invisible to the stack map while `mine("jjjj")` allocates. Once this program
shares the live heap, that allocation can collect, and `x`'s buffer goes with
it.

The obvious repair is not available. Reading `x` BEFORE allocating `y` would
let an implementation that hands back one shared buffer pass, which is exactly
the bug B3 exists to catch - so the raw address MUST span the allocation. And
there is nowhere to root it: every port object here is an `int`, including the
`rtSliceNew` handle inside `mine`, so the program has no reference-typed slot
to hold anything in. That is deliberate; it tests the object model below the
type system.

Same shape as ../rootbit's waiver, and the same accounting: until G3 this
program owned a private heap the live runtime collector had never been told about,
so nothing it allocated was ever a collection candidate. BIT_GC=off is not a
downgrade from coverage that existed - it is the coverage it always had, now
labelled. The default-policy run still asserts all 31 properties and still has
to match the golden.

Note what this waiver does NOT cover: the deterministic bridge=0/31 and the
intermittent SIGSEGV that #1845 fixed were a different defect entirely - an
unguarded rootInit whose rootBindMemory had already repointed the live
collector's heap. Those are fixed in the code, not waived here.
